--- /dev/null
+++ b/fs/filesystems/procfs/docs/security.md
@@ -0,0 +1,39 @@
+# procfs — Security and Reliability
+
+## Trust model and boundaries
+
+Procfs reads kernel-owned task metadata and address-space accounting. User input
+is limited to path components and existing command files such as
+`oom_score_adj`; status/statm use no user pointers. The memory snapshot exposes
+numeric sizes and backing categories, never mapped page contents.
+
+## Memory and lifecycle invariants
+
+- A per-task closure upgrades its weak task reference for each read and cannot
+  dereference a reclaimed task.
+- A live MM capability is held only while taking the snapshot, then dropped
+  before formatting or returning bytes.
+- The MM lock serializes VMA/PTE accounting changes with snapshot reads.
+- An address space without a live mm produces seven zero statm fields; status
+  omits its memory block.
+
+## Failure handling
+
+Task lookup/read races use the existing ENOENT/ESRCH VFS mappings. Invalid or
+unsupported file operations return typed VFS errors; statm/status do not turn a
+failed snapshot into guessed numbers.
+
+## Privacy and known limitations
+
+The current procfs implementation does not provide the full Linux hidepid and
+ptrace-based authorization model. This change adds only numeric memory usage to
+the existing procfs task view and does not expand mapped-content access. Full
+status fields and authorization parity are out of scope.
+
+## Audit checklist
+
+- Check that new nodes are registered in both task-directory paths as intended.
+- Keep procfs reads outside process-domain/member spinlocks before taking MM locks.
+- Confirm statm fields remain in Linux order and units.
+- Confirm no status/statm path reads or faults user memory.
+- Keep no-mm and exited-task outcomes distinct.
