 - `TaskStat`, `system_view`, nice/scheduler and CPU-accounting APIs provide
   procfs/scheduler snapshots rather than owning hardware scheduling policy.
 
+`TaskStat::from_thread()` also snapshots current `MmMemoryUsage` while holding a
+`LiveAddressSpace` capability and the MM mutex. It exposes current virtual bytes,
+resident base pages, and backing-category counts for procfs formatting. It does
+not retain the MM handle after the snapshot. Exec VMA metadata is used to derive
+the main executable's code/data bounds and the user stack/heap anchors used by
+the statm formatter.
+
+The current `statm.text` adapter counts executable VMAs belonging to the
+process executable path. `statm.data` counts private writable VMAs, which is an
+X-Kernel approximation of Linux `data_vm + stack_vm`; it does not yet carry
+per-ELF-segment data/stack accounting through every exec path.
+
 CPU accounting uses `Inactive`, `Kernel`, and `User` states, following Linux
 generic vtime. Switch-out settles the interval; switch-in starts kernel time.
 User transitions are marked at `UserContext::run` boundaries before IRQ dispatch.
